"About 2,500 years ago, the skill of making iron reached Taiwan. At the Shihsanhang Site, near the north coast, which dates from about 1,800 years ago, archaeologists found a furnace and a great deal of iron slag, the waste left over from smelting. They also found glass, gold, and agate ornaments that came from outside the island, a sign of frequent trade. People did not live only near the sea. At Qubing, high in the mountains of Nantou, radiocarbon dates show that people were living there between about 1,000 and more than 2,700 years ago.": "f965448a8b39",
"Archaeologists divide Taiwan’s long prehistory into three ages, named for what people made their tools from. In the Paleolithic, or Old Stone Age, people made stone tools only by chipping, and they did not make pottery. In the Neolithic, or New Stone Age, people ground and polished their stone tools, made pottery, and began to farm. In the Iron Age, people learned to make iron. These ages did not begin on one day. They are names that archaeologists use to put thousands of finds in order.": "19073cd050b0",
"Archaeologists excavate slowly and record everything.": "0f43d28fb028",
"Archaeologists have found more than 1,500 slate coffins at the Peinan Site.": "08198d7078d5",
"At Peinan, a burial was a coffin made of slate slabs.": "9a48c85242d2",
"By about 1,800 years ago, people here were smelting iron.": "8ac1f112bb22",
"By about 3,500 years ago, some villages had grown large. At the Peinan Site in Taitung, houses stood in planned rows. Archaeologists have found a great many coffins there, made of flat slabs of slate: more than 2,000 by the museum’s count. Some of the dead were buried with pots and with jade ornaments. The museum says the people of Peinan got their jade from more than 100 kilometers away. One small jade earring from a Peinan coffin, carved with two people and an animal, is now a National Treasure.": "36a5b5ea5828",
"Glass beads are a sign of trade with other places.": "72183ef1cee1",
"In what is now a park in Taipei, archaeologists found mounds made mostly of shells. About three thousand years ago, people who lived here ate shellfish and threw the shells away in the same place. Animal bones, fish bones, broken pots, and worn-out stone tools went onto the pile too. It was their trash heap, and more than that: they also buried their dead in it. It is called the Yuanshan shell mound. Building work has destroyed most of it, but it is still one of the most important sites in northern Taiwan. Nobody wrote a word about these people, yet what they left tells us what they ate and what they made.": "ceda62957266",
"In what is now a park in Taipei, archaeologists found mounds made mostly of shells. About three thousand years ago, people who lived here ate shellfish and threw the shells away in the same place. Animal bones, fish bones, broken pots, and worn-out stone tools went onto the pile too. It was their trash heap, and more than that: they also buried their dead in it. It is called the Yuanshan shell mound. Building work has destroyed most of it, but it is still one of the most important sites in northern Taiwan. Nobody wrote a word about these people, yet what they left tells us what they ate and what they made. Archaeologists divide Taiwan’s long prehistory into three ages, named for what people made their tools from. In the Paleolithic, or Old Stone Age, people made stone tools only by chipping, and they did not make pottery. In the Neolithic, or New Stone Age, people ground and polished their stone tools, made pottery, and began to farm. In the Iron Age, people learned to make iron. These ages did not begin on one day. They are names that archaeologists use to put thousands of finds in order. The oldest culture found so far is the Changbin Culture. At the Baxian Caves on the east coast, people lived in caves beside the sea. They hunted and gathered their food, and they left stone tools and the remains of fires. Then, about 6,000 to 5,000 years ago, according to the National Museum of Prehistory, another group of people arrived by sea from the southeast coast of the Asian mainland. They brought pottery and polished stone tools, and they had some knowledge of farming. Many scholars think so, the museum adds, although no boats from that time have been found. Archaeologists call their culture Dapenkeng, after a site in what is now New Taipei City. By about 3,500 years ago, some villages had grown large. At the Peinan Site in Taitung, houses stood in planned rows. Archaeologists have found a great many coffins there, made of flat slabs of slate: more than 2,000 by the museum’s count. Some of the dead were buried with pots and with jade ornaments. The museum says the people of Peinan got their jade from more than 100 kilometers away. One small jade earring from a Peinan coffin, carved with two people and an animal, is now a National Treasure. About 2,500 years ago, the skill of making iron reached Taiwan. At the Shihsanhang Site, near the north coast, which dates from about 1,800 years ago, archaeologists found a furnace and a great deal of iron slag, the waste left over from smelting. They also found glass, gold, and agate ornaments that came from outside the island, a sign of frequent trade. People did not live only near the sea. At Qubing, high in the mountains of Nantou, radiocarbon dates show that people were living there between about 1,000 and more than 2,700 years ago. So who were all these people? That is harder to answer than it sounds. Pots and tools do not tell us what language people spoke or what they called themselves. The National Museum of Prehistory says there is no direct evidence that the Peinan Culture was handed down to the Pinuyumayan people of today. For some Iron Age cultures, it says only that they may be related to Indigenous cultures. A careful historian says “may be” here, and keeps looking. Lesson 3 follows this question further.": "9b07220f4058",
"So who were all these people? That is harder to answer than it sounds. Pots and tools do not tell us what language people spoke or what they called themselves. The National Museum of Prehistory says there is no direct evidence that the Peinan Culture was handed down to the Pinuyumayan people of today. For some Iron Age cultures, it says only that they may be related to Indigenous cultures. A careful historian says “may be” here, and keeps looking. Lesson 3 follows this question further.": "35e219344937",
